      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the possible degrees of the remainder when a polynomial is divided by \\(2x^2 - 7x + 18\\), we need to understand the polynomial division process and the relationship between the degrees of the dividend, divisor, and quotient.\n\nGiven:\n- Polynomial: \\(P(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\\)\n- Divisor: \\(D(x) = 2x^2 - 7x + 18\\)\n\nWhen we divide \\(P(x)\\) by \\(D(x)\\), the result is:\n\\[ \\text{Quotient} \\cdot D(x) + R \\]\nWhere \\(R\\) is the remainder.\n\nThe degree of the quotient must be 1 or 0, because:\n- If the degree of the remainder is greater than the degree of the divisor (2), the degree of the remainder would be greater than that of \\(D(x)\\), making the division impossible (degree-wise).\n\nThus:\n1. If the degree of the remainder is 0, \\(D(x)\\) would completely divide \\(P(x)\\), which implies \\(P(x)\\) is a perfectly divisible form under \\(D(x)\\).\n2. If the degree of the remainder is 1, the highest term in \\(P(x)\\) would have to be dominated enough by the degree of \\(D(x)\\) (i.e., \\(2x^2 - 7x + 18\\)), making a form impossible without another polynomial to adjust for highest terms by multiplying it appropriately.\n\nNow we will illustrate using polynomial division the feasibility for each degree of remainder possible for when \\(2x^2 - 7x + 18\\) is the divisor.\n\nFor the degree of the remainder to be 0:\n\\[ \\deg(R) = 0 \\]\nDividing \\((ax^2 + bx + c) \\div (2x^2 - 7x + 18)\\) and there cannot be a remainder. The numerator must be exactly divisible by the denominator (no remainder) then. \\(x^2 \\div (2x) \\text{ or } \\frac{1}{2} x^2 \\text { is } 0 \\).\n\nFor the degree of the remainder to be 1:\n\\[ \\deg(R) = 1 \\]\nThere must be a P \\( \\text{ possible } remainder in the highest term divisible by the divisor. For a function,\n\\[ P(x) = a (2x^2 - 7x + 18) = 2ax^2 - 7ax + 18a , \\text{ have 0 degree remainder (divisibility) } \\text given } R(x) \\ne 0 \\]\n\nThis proves:\n\\[ \\boxed{0}, [1] \\] is the possible degree forms of the quotient and the degree of remainder possible in this polynomial problem.\nUsers please sign in to add new comments.\n\n@above huh? That's too much experience, isn't it? I know that $2x^2$ is the highest term in $2x^2 - 7x + 18$, but how height are the terms of the polynomial? Can the answer be 1,5?\n\nIf you'd found $P(x) = (2x^2-7x+18)Q(x)+x$ that works if $P(x)$ has degree $1$. It works out for any degree $P(x)$. You can have polynomial $P$ and polynomial divisor $D$ and have any degree of remainder as long as $P(x)$ is bigger (or the same degree) than the divisor.",
